  "text": "Comparative Oncology Research – Flint Animal Cancer Center\nSkip to content\ncolorado state\nuniversity\nCSU\nflint animal\ncancer center\nAbout Us\nOur Story\nFACC Team\nContact Us\nNews\nFor Owners\nOur Services\nCancer Information Request\nAppointments\nTreatment Options\nPreparing for Your Visit\nFrequently Asked Questions\nFor DVMs\nConsult Line\nTreatment Options\nOne Cure\nAbout One Cure\nRacing for One Cure\nDonate to One Cure\nClinical Trials\nAbout Clinical Trials\nCurrent Clinical Trials\nEducation & Research\nComparative Oncology Research\nOncology Specialty Training\nDonate\nGreatest Need\nPatient Assistance\nOne Cure\nScholarship\nResearch\nFeline Cancer Research\nFACC Friends\nmenu\nclose\nTag:\nComparative Oncology Research\nsearch\nread more\nPet Cancer Treatment\nUsing 3-D Printing Technology To Personalize Limb-Spare Implants\nLauren Mingus\n|\nJuly 11, 2019\nOne of the most common cancers in dogs is osteosarcoma (bone cancer). Typical treatment includes amputation, but for some patients, amputation is not an option due to neurologic or orthopedic issues, such as arthritis. The limb-spare technique developed by Flint Animal Cancer Center’s founding director Dr. Steve Withrow provided a solution for those patients (and human […]\nread more\nResearch\nComparative Oncology Research: Accelerating Discovery for Pets and People\nLauren Mingus\n|\nMay 5, 2022\nMay is National Cancer Research Month, launched in 2007 by the American Association for Cancer Research to highlight the importance of lifesaving cancer research. For more than 40 years, the Flint Animal Cancer Center has been at the forefront of comparative oncology research and discovery to benefit pets and people with cancer. In line with […]\nread more\nLatest News\nThe Flint Animal Cancer Center Advances Precision Veterinary Cancer Care\nKate Shelton\n|\nApril 22, 2026\nColorado State University’s Flint Animal Cancer Center has reached an important milestone in its mission to deliver world-class cancer treatment for companion animals. The new Varian Edge linear accelerator is now open and treating patients. This state-of-the-art system is designed to deliver highly precise and efficient radiation therapy for pets fighting cancer.  This advanced technology enhances the FACC’s ability to treat cancer with increased accuracy while minimizing impact on […]\nread more\nFACC Patient Stories\nFang’s Clinical Trial Story\nKate Shelton\n|\nMarch 6, 2025\nIf your pet is diagnosed with cancer, the first thing you will do is find the best treatment options for your best friend. For many, the best option is a clinical trial. Clinical trials provide access to new, cutting-edge treatments that will offer your pet a fighting chance. They also give cancer researchers important insights […]\nread more\nResearch\nCancer research to benefit pets and people\nLauren Mingus\n|\nJune 2, 2021\nFor nearly four decades, the Flint Animal Cancer Center has been at the forefront of understanding the fundamental mechanisms of cancer biology, disease diagnosis, and the development of effective therapies. Our focus is on comparative oncology to improve the prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of cancer in pet animals, and translate our research and knowledge also to […]\nread more\nLatest News\nFACC scientists search for solutions to cancer treatment challenges\nLauren Mingus\n|\nMay 25, 2021\nWhile many scientists at the Flint Animal Cancer Center focus their research on cancer cells, the Laboratory of Comparative Musculoskeletal Oncology and Traumatology views cancer through an additional lens. “Many of our colleagues are doing great work focusing on cancer itself – how to treat it effectively, how to make sure it doesn’t spread,” said […]\nread more\nLatest News\nThe answers to cancer start at the cellular level.\nLauren Mingus\n|\nMay 3, 2021\n“Cancer is really a process associated with basic biology,” said Dr. Daniel Gustafson, director of basic research and the Pharmacology Lab at the Flint Animal Cancer Center. “It’s about the cells in your body that for some reason have changed and grown out of control.” The solutions to most problems start by looking at the […]\nread more\nResearch\nConquering Cat Cancer: Feline Researchers at the FACC\nKate Shelton\n|\nFebruary 4, 2025\nFeline cancer is all too common. In fact, as many as one in five cats will be diagnosed with cancer in their life, and many will succumb to this disease. Despite the grim prognosis of feline cancer, this cancer in cats is underfunded and underresearched in cats. Here at the Flint Animal Cancer Center, we’re […]\nbrowse articles by topic\nFACC Patient Stories\nLatest News\nOne Cure Clinical Trials\nOne Cure Patients\nOur Team\nPet Cancer Treatment\nResearch\nStories of Hope\nTeaching and Learning\nbrowse articles by tag\nBone Cancer\nBrain Tumor\nCancer Research\nCancer Stories\nCanine Cancer\nCanine Cancer Clinical Trials\nCanine Cancer Prevention Vaccine\ncanine clinical trial\nCanine Hemangiosarcoma\nCanine Leg Amputation\nCanine Lymphoma\nCanine Nasal Tumor\nCanine Osteosarcoma\nCanine Soft Tissue Sarcoma canine cancer clinical trials\nCaring for pets with cancer\ncat cancer\nChemotherapy\ncomparative oncology\nComparative Oncology Research\nDan Regan\nDiagnostic Imaging\nDiet for Pets with Cancer\nEmotional Support\nfeline cancer\nFeline GI Lymphoma\nFeline Lymphoma\nFlint Animal Cancer Center Staff\nGlioma\nGolden Retriever Lifetime Study\nHemangiosarcoma\nLaiks Fund\nlimb-spare surgery\nNasal Osteosarcoma\nNews\nOne Cure\nOne Health\nPatient Assistance Funds\nPatient Care\nPatient Stories\nPatient Story\nPet Cancer\nPet Cancer Comparative Oncology\nPet Cancer Patient Assistance Funds\nPet Cancer Prevention\nPet Cancer Terms\nPet Cancer Treatment\nPet Grief Counseling\npet leg amputation\nPet Loss\nPet Quality of Life\nPlasmacytomas\nRacing for One Cure\nradiation biology\nRadiation Bolus\nRadiation Oncology Research\nRadiation Therapy\nradioimmunology\nRe